{:db/id 17592186048343 :topic/title "Consistency and Dependency in Linear Systems" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id 17592186198346 :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Consistency and Dependency Terminology" :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186198224 :question/math-academy-id "e-15010" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables, where each equation is represented by the corresponding straight line in the plane. What can we say about the intersections of these lines, given that the system is\n\n1. inconsistent,\n2. consistent independent, or\n3. consistent dependent?" :question/worked-solution "The solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations. Three possibilities exist, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c544df38ce82dd70c3d01718233c9fae1fae0534748892643a12b6556918fb7.png)\n\nIn other words, we have the following cases:\n\n- If a system is *inconsistent* (has no solutions), the corresponding lines are parallel (do not intersect). And vice versa, parallel lines correspond to an inconsistent system.\n- If a system is *consistent independent* (i.e., has exactly one solution), the corresponding lines intersect at exactly one point. And vice versa, intersecting lines correspond to a consistent independent system.\n- If a system is *consistent dependent* (i.e., has infinitely many solutions), the corresponding lines intersect at infinitely many points (coincide). And vice versa, coinciding lines correspond to a consistent dependent system."} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186198928 :question/math-academy-id "q-223036" :question/problem "A system of linear equations with two variables, $x$ and $y$, is inconsistent. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe system has {{field-1}} and the lines corresponding to the equations {{field-2}}."} {:db/id 17592186198936 :question/math-academy-id "q-237024" :question/problem "A system of linear equations has infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the system is"} {:db/id 17592186567728 :question/problem "A system of two linear equations with two variables, $x$ and $y$, is inconsistent. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe system has {{field-1}} and the lines corresponding to the equations {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "An inconsistent system has no solutions.\n\nThe solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations.\n\nThe lines therefore do not intersect: they are parallel (non-intersecting)."} {:db/id 17592186567729 :question/problem "A system of two linear equations with two variables, $x$ and $y$, is consistent dependent. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe system has {{field-1}} and the lines corresponding to the equations {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A consistent dependent system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nThe solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations.\n\nThe lines therefore intersect at infinitely many points: they coincide with each other."} {:db/id 17592186567747 :question/problem "A system of linear equations has infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the system is" :question/worked-solution "The system has at least one solution, so it is consistent. Because it has infinitely many solutions, it is consistent dependent."} {:db/id 17592186567749 :question/problem "A system of linear equations has no solutions. Therefore, the system is" :question/worked-solution "A system is consistent if it has at least one solution. This system has no solutions, so it is inconsistent."} {:db/id 17592186567755 :question/problem "A system of linear equations has exactly one solution. Therefore, the system is" :question/worked-solution "The system has a solution, so it is consistent. Because it has exactly one solution, it is consistent independent."}]} {:db/id 17592186198347 :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Slope-Intercept Systems Using Consistency and Dependency Terminology" :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186198225 :question/math-academy-id "e-15011" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2x + 6 \\\\ y &=2x + 1\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nComment on the consistency of this system, the corresponding number of solutions, and its geometric interpretation." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nThe solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations. Three possibilities exist, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c544df38ce82dd70c3d01718233c9fae1fae0534748892643a12b6556918fb7.png)\n\nNotice that the equations in the system have the same slopes ($2$) but distinct $y$-intercepts ($6$ and $1$, respectively). So, the lines $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{are parallel}}$ and do not intersect.\n\nTherefore, the system has $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{no solutions}}$, meaning that it's $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{inconsistent}}$."} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186198929 :question/math-academy-id "q-223043" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 - 2x \\\\ y &=-2x + 1\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nSelect the correct options in the following reasonong.\n\nThe lines corresponding to the equations {{field-1}}. Therefore, the system has {{field-2}}, meaning that it's {{field-3}}."} {:db/id 17592186198937 :question/math-academy-id "q-237129" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=6x + 5 \\\\ y &=5x + 6\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nThe lines corresponding to the equations {{field-1}}. Therefore, the system has {{field-2}}, meaning that it's {{field-3}}."} {:db/id 17592186567774 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 - 4x \\\\ y &=-4x + 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nThe lines corresponding to the equations {{field-1}}. Therefore, the system has {{field-2}}, meaning that it's {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nThe solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations. Three possibilities exist, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c544df38ce82dd70c3d01718233c9fae1fae0534748892643a12b6556918fb7.png)\n\nNotice that the equations in the system have the same slopes ($-4$) and the same $y$-intercepts ($1$). So, the lines $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{coincide with each other}}$ and share every point.\n\nTherefore, the system has $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{infinitely many solutions}}$, meaning that it's $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{consistent dependent}}$."} {:db/id 17592186567786 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2x + 2 \\\\ y &=4x + 8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nThe lines corresponding to the equations {{field-1}}. Therefore, the system has {{field-2}}, meaning that it's {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nThe solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations. Three possibilities exist, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c544df38ce82dd70c3d01718233c9fae1fae0534748892643a12b6556918fb7.png)\n\nNotice that the equations in the system have distinct slopes ($-2$ and $4$, respectively). So, the lines $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{intersect at one point}}$.\n\nTherefore, the system has $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{only one solution}}$, meaning that it's $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{consistent independent}}$."} {:db/id 17592186567796 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=4x + 9 \\\\ y &=4x + 9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nThe lines corresponding to the equations {{field-1}}. Therefore, the system has {{field-2}}, meaning that it's {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nThe solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations. Three possibilities exist, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c544df38ce82dd70c3d01718233c9fae1fae0534748892643a12b6556918fb7.png)\n\nNotice that the equations in the system have the same slopes ($4$) and the same $y$-intercepts ($9$). So, the lines $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{coincide with each other}}$ and share every point.\n\nTherefore, the system has $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{infinitely many solutions}}$, meaning that it's $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{consistent dependent}}$."} {:db/id 17592186567819 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-9 + 7x \\\\ y &=8x - 7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nThe lines corresponding to the equations {{field-1}}. Therefore, the system has {{field-2}}, meaning that it's {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nThe solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations. Three possibilities exist, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c544df38ce82dd70c3d01718233c9fae1fae0534748892643a12b6556918fb7.png)\n\nNotice that the equations in the system have distinct slopes ($7$ and $8$, respectively). So, the lines $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{intersect at one point}}$.\n\nTherefore, the system has $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{only one solution}}$, meaning that it's $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{consistent independent}}$."} {:db/id 17592186567826 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=4x - 5 \\\\ y &=4x + 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nThe lines corresponding to the equations {{field-1}}. Therefore, the system has {{field-2}}, meaning that it's {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nThe solutions of a linear system with two variables correspond to the points of intersection of the two lines defined by the equations. Three possibilities exist, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c544df38ce82dd70c3d01718233c9fae1fae0534748892643a12b6556918fb7.png)\n\nNotice that the equations in the system have the same slopes ($4$) but distinct $y$-intercepts ($-5$ and $1$, respectively). So, the lines $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{are parallel}}$ and do not intersect.\n\nTherefore, the system has $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{no solutions}}$, meaning that it's $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{inconsistent}}$."}]} {:db/id 17592186198348 :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems Using Consistency and Dependency Terminology" :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186198220 :question/math-academy-id "e-14017" :question/problem "Determine whether the following system of equations is consistent or inconsistent. If it is consistent, indicate whether it is consistent dependent or consistent independent.\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y - 2 &=0 \\\\ 4x - 8y &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y - 2 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 2 + 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\nx=2+2y\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 8y &= 8 \\\\\n4(2 + 2y) - 8y &= 8 \\\\\n8 + 8y - 8y &= 8 \\\\\n8 &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the system is consistent dependent."} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186198914 :question/math-academy-id "q-211985" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x - 3y &=-3 \\\\ - 2x + y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}."} {:db/id 17592186198938 :question/math-academy-id "q-237133" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x &=4 + 2y \\\\ 3y &=9 - 12x\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}."} {:db/id 17592186567832 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x - 3y &=-9 \\\\ -2x - 3y &=-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x - 3y &= -9 \\\\ -2x &= -9 + 3y \\\\ x &= \\frac{9}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{9}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x - 3y &= -9 \\\\ -2\\left(\\frac{9}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}y\\right) - 3y &= -9 \\\\ -9 + 3y - 3y &= -9 \\\\ -9 &= -9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the system is consistent dependent."} {:db/id 17592186567834 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x &=7 - 3y \\\\ 3y &=8 - 3x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x &= 7 - 3y \\\\ x &= \\frac{7}{3} - y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{7}{3} - y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y &= 8 - 3x \\\\ 3y &= 8 - 3\\left(\\frac{7}{3} - y\\right) \\\\ 3y &= 8 - 7 + 3y \\\\ 0 &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a false statement, we conclude that there are no solutions to the system. Therefore, the system is inconsistent."} {:db/id 17592186567836 :question/problem "Determine whether the following system of equations is consistent or inconsistent. If it is consistent, indicate whether it is consistent dependent or consistent independent.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6x - 4y - 9 &=0 \\\\ -12x - 8y &=19\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x - 4y - 9 &= 0 \\\\ -6x &= 9 + 4y \\\\ x &= -\\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{2}{3}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=-\\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{2}{3}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x - 8y &= 19 \\\\ -12\\left(-\\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{2}{3}y\\right) - 8y &= 19 \\\\ 18 + 8y - 8y &= 19 \\\\ 18 &= 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a false statement, we conclude that there are no solutions to the system. Therefore, the system is inconsistent."} {:db/id 17592186567838 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x + 3y &=3 \\\\ 2x + 4y &=-24\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x + 3y &= 3 \\\\ -4x &= 3 - 3y \\\\ x &= -\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{3}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=-\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{3}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x + 4y &= -24 \\\\ 2\\left(-\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{3}{4}y\\right) + 4y &= -24 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{3}{2}y + 4y &= -24 \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}y &= -\\frac{45}{2} \\\\ y &= -\\frac{45}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a single value of $y$, and $x$ is determined by $x=-\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{3}{4}y$, there is exactly one solution to the system. Therefore, the system is consistent independent."} {:db/id 17592186567847 :question/problem "Determine whether the following system of equations is consistent or inconsistent. If it is consistent, indicate whether it is consistent dependent or consistent independent.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x - 4y + 11 &=0 \\\\ 5x - 8y &=23\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system of equations is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A system of equations is *consistent* if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, it is called *inconsistent*.\n\nConsistent systems, in turn, can be of the following two types:\n\n- When a system of equations has a *single* solution, it is called *consistent independent*.\n- When a system of equations has an *infinite number* of solutions, it is called *consistent dependent*.\n\nWe can summarize these definitions using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution.\n\nFirst, we can solve the first equation for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x - 4y + 11 &= 0 \\\\ 5x &= -11 + 4y \\\\ x &= -\\frac{11}{5} + \\frac{4}{5}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=-\\frac{11}{5} + \\frac{4}{5}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation to obtain an equation that we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x - 8y &= 23 \\\\ 5\\left(-\\frac{11}{5} + \\frac{4}{5}y\\right) - 8y &= 23 \\\\ -11 + 4y - 8y &= 23 \\\\ -4y &= 34 \\\\ y &= -\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a single value of $y$, and $x$ is determined by $x=-\\frac{11}{5} + \\frac{4}{5}y$, there is exactly one solution to the system. Therefore, the system is consistent independent."}]}]}
